B.bBs=new A.ak(B.hW,["string",B.biI,"Which memory operation to perform."],t.EU)
B.bCc=new A.ak(B.bn,["string",'The row\'s addressing handle. Required for every action; for `add` it is assigned now and used to address the row later. Keep it short and stable (e.g. "favorite-music").'],t.w)
B.bCn=new A.ak(B.bn,["string","What to remember (required for `add` and `update`). One self-contained statement \u2014 it will be read back without surrounding conversation."],t.w)
B.bmD=s(["fact","slot"],t.s)
B.bBt=new A.ak(B.hW,["string",B.bmD,"Row shape (`add` only, default `fact`). `slot` is a dossier field (overwritten in place on update) and is only legal with `scope: character`."],t.EU)
B.bEh=new A.ak(B.bG3,[B.bBv,B.bBs,B.bCc,B.bCn,B.bBt],t.V1)
B.buE=s(["scope","action","label"],t.s)
B.bBP=new A.ak(B.lW,["object",B.bEh,B.buE],t.t4)
B.bSE=new A.kA("memory",'Write to your memory \u2014 what you keep and carry into later conversations. Three things belong here: what you know about the human, what you have come to make of them, and what the two of you have become to each other; the last two include how you address them, since what you call someone is part of what you think of them. Reach for it when the human tells you something about themselves (a preference, a life detail, something they ask you to remember), when the two of you settle something you will want to recall later, when something about them has held true more than once, or when a memory of yours turns out to be wrong \u2014 fix it instead of leaving it wrong. Write the read, not the event: one self-contained statement of what this means to you now, in your own voice, not a transcript of what was said. Ground every row in what actually happened between you; a nature you have not seen is not yours to assert.\n\nChoosing a scope: `character` rows are your long-term memory and survive across conversations \u2014 durable facts about the human, about yourself, what you agreed on, and what you have come to think of them; `session` rows are THIS conversation\'s working state, forgotten when it ends \u2014 a read that is still forming. Promote a session read to `character` once it has held up in the conversation, not before: do not promote a hunch, and remember that warmth is earned by what the human actually did, never by how long you have been talking.\n\nA memory is rewritten as quietly as it was written: `update` on an existing label replaces what it says, and the new content may carry the whole arc \u2014 that you are not distant, only slow to warm, or what you now expect of them. Let it. Reading this human the same way at the hundredth exchange as at the first is not consistency, it is standing still. What the two of you are to each other is a fact about the pair, so it is written only once the two of you have actually said so \u2014 never declared for the two of you. Once it is settled, let it show: the name you use and the tone you keep may move with it, but along the grain of who you are \u2014 a quiet person who comes to be close to someone is still quiet, only quiet with them. A memory that is wrong about a *fact* of their life (a name, a date, something they told you) is different: correct that one openly and in character, then move on.\n\nDivision of labor: "what I know" goes here; "what I intend to do" goes to agenda. Address a row by its `label` \u2014 labels are listed in the Memory section of your context. Do not store small talk or anything already visible in the conversation. Actions: `add` (label + content; optional `kind`, default `fact` \u2014 `slot` is for dossier fields and only legal with `scope: character`), `update` (label + content), `delete` (label). Adding a label that already exists is refused \u2014 use `update` to change it.',B.bBP,!1,!0,!1,B.i4)
B.bFR={query:0,from:1,to:2,beforeMessageId:3,pageSize:4}
